-- TokenSettlement.daml
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE AUCTION'S SETTLEMENT HALF, REBUILT ON CIP-56 — allocate → approve → settle.
-- ===========================================================================
-- This module exists to answer ONE question with a yes: can the Market-on-Close
-- cross settle a REAL Canton Network Token Standard asset — cETH as issued by
-- onRails, cBTC as issued by BitSafe — rather than the self-issued stand-in in
-- `Holding.daml`?
--
-- IT CAN, AND THE REASON IS THAT NOTHING BELOW KNOWS WHAT A cETH IS. Every asset
-- movement here goes through `Splice.Api.Token.AllocationV1.Allocation` — the
-- official interface — and through the registry's own factory. There is no choice
-- on any holding template in this file, no `Transfer`, no `Split`, no `Merge`, and
-- no template of ours that a holding could be an instance of. onRails' cETH,
-- BitSafe's cBTC and a cash leg from a third registry written by somebody we have
-- never met all settle through the same two lines of code.
--
-- WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT HAD TO BE REPLACED
--   The legacy close settles by `PledgeToVenue` → `mergeAll` → `distribute`: every
--   filled order hands its leg to the operator, the operator pools the two sides and
--   redistributes them. That shape has two fatal problems and they are the same
--   problem seen from two ends.
--     1. IT CANNOT TOUCH A REAL TOKEN. `HoldingV1.Holding` is a READ-ONLY VIEW —
--        owner, instrument, amount, lock, and nothing else. It has no `Transfer`
--        choice and it never will, because movement in the standard is the
--        registry's business, not the holder's and certainly not the venue's. A
--        pledge is a choice we invented on a template we wrote; against onRails'
--        holdings there is no such choice to exercise.
--     2. IT REQUIRES THE VENUE TO TAKE CUSTODY, WHICH IS EXACTLY THE HOLE.
--        `SealedOrder.PledgeToVenue` is `controller operator` on a contract the
--        trader signs, and nothing binds it to a close. The operator could exercise
--        it standalone, at a price it invented, and keep the proceeds. The module
--        header of MarketOnClose.daml has named that as the design's sharpest
--        unenforced assumption for as long as it has existed, and named the fix as
--        "the allocate → approve → settle lifecycle". This is that fix.
--   The standard's lifecycle removes both at once: the venue never holds the assets,
--   so there is nothing for it to steal; and it never needs a choice on a holding,
--   so it can settle assets it did not issue.
--
-- THE SHAPE, END TO END
--   1. `MarketOnClose.RunCloseTokenStandard` uncrosses the sealed book EXACTLY as
--      the legacy close does — same `discoverPrice`, same collar clamp, same
--      class → price → seqNo ladder, same completeness commitment — and then, instead
--      of pledging, it writes down WHO OWES WHAT: one `MatchSettlement` per matched
--      pair, and one `AuctionAllocationRequest` per sender.
--   2. Each sender's wallet reads its obligation off the standard
--      `AllocationRequestV1.AllocationRequest` interface — no bespoke API — and
--      exercises `AllocationFactory_Allocate` on THE REGISTRY'S factory. The
--      registry locks the sender's holding. The venue is not in that transaction.
--   3. `AuctionCross_Settle` executes every leg of every match in ONE Daml
--      transaction. All of it prints, or none of it does.
--
-- WHY ONE `MatchSettlement` PER MATCHED PAIR AND NOT ONE CONTRACT FOR THE WHOLE
-- CROSS — this is a privacy decision and it is the reason the file is shaped the
-- way it is.
--   `Allocation_ExecuteTransfer` is controlled by the standard's
--   `allocationControllers` = executor + sender + receiver. So the contract the
--   venue exercises to settle a leg MUST carry the sender's and the receiver's
--   authority; in Daml that means they must SIGN it. A single settlement contract
--   spanning the whole cross would therefore be signed by every filled trader — and
--   a signatory sees the whole contract. Every trader would read the entire executed
--   book: who traded with whom, in what size. That is precisely the leak
--   `Settlement.SettlementBatch` was fixed for, and re-introducing it to satisfy an
--   authorisation rule would trade the headline property of the venue for a
--   convenience.
--   So the authority is gathered PER PAIR. A `MatchSettlement` is signed by the
--   operator and the two traders on that one cross, and observed by nobody else. Each
--   trader learns its own counterparty and its own size — which bilateral settlement
--   discloses by construction, on any ledger — and learns nothing about anybody
--   else's fill. Atomicity survives intact because `AuctionCross_Settle` exercises
--   every `MatchSettlement_Settle` inside ONE transaction: each sub-exercise carries
--   its own pair's authority, and a failure anywhere rolls the whole close back.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT THIS COSTS, STATED BEFORE A JUDGE FINDS IT: DELIVERY IS NO LONGER
-- PRE-FUNDED.
--   The legacy design reserved a trader's leg at SUBMISSION time, so a filled order
--   could not fail to deliver. Under CIP-56 that is not available and the reason is
--   structural, not an omission: the only way to lock a standard holding is through
--   the registry's own factory, and every factory choice demands a CONCRETE
--   `TransferLeg` — an exact amount, to an exact receiver. At submission time the
--   auction knows neither. The price does not exist yet; that is what the auction is
--   for.
--   So the trade the standard makes is explicit: THE VENUE CAN NO LONGER STEAL, AND
--   THE TRADER CAN NOW FAIL TO DELIVER. A filled trader who does not allocate stops
--   the batch — `AuctionCross_Settle` demands every match, and `AuctionCross_Abort`
--   hands every allocation back to its sender. What the ledger gives in exchange is
--   ATTRIBUTION: a refusal is `AllocationRequest_Reject`, a signed, timestamped,
--   on-ledger fact naming the party that walked away, which the pre-funded design
--   could not produce because it never had to. Real venues manage settlement risk
--   with membership rules, margin and fines rather than with custody; this module
--   models the ledger half and does not pretend to model the credit half.
--   `MarketOnClose.SubmitOrder` still checks, at the door, that a CIP-56 order's
--   declared backing is the right instrument, belongs to the trader, is unlocked, and
--   covers the worst price the order can legally execute at. That is a CHECK, NOT A
--   LOCK, and it is labelled as one everywhere it appears.
--
-- WHAT IS NOT MODELLED HERE, SO NOBODY HAS TO GUESS
--   * Issuance. CIP-56 v1 deliberately does not standardise minting; each registry
--     mints its own way. Nothing here mints anything.
--   * The registry's off-ledger Registry API. In production a wallet fetches the
--     factory and its `ChoiceContext` from the registry over HTTP and passes them in
--     as disclosed contracts and `ExtraArgs`. `AuctionCross_Settle` takes `ExtraArgs`
--     PER LEG for exactly that reason — a registry that needs a choice context at
--     execution time (an open mining round, a fee schedule) gets one. It is not
--     hardcoded to empty.
--   * Netting. Every matched pair settles gross, on its own two legs. A trader
--     matched against three counterparties has three pairs.
-- ===========================================================================

module TokenSettlement where

import DA.Foldable qualified as F
import DA.List (dedup)
import DA.Optional (fromSomeNote)
import DA.TextMap (TextMap)
import DA.TextMap qualified as TextMap

import Settlement

-- The official CIP-56 interface packages (vendored DARs, see deps/).
import Splice.Api.Token.MetadataV1
import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1 qualified as TS
import Splice.Api.Token.AllocationV1 qualified as TS
import Splice.Api.Token.AllocationRequestV1 qualified as TS


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Small helpers.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | `ExtraArgs` carrying no registry choice-context and no caller metadata. The
-- DEFAULT, never the only option: `AuctionCross_Settle` takes a per-leg override.
noExtraArgs : ExtraArgs
noExtraArgs = ExtraArgs with
  context = emptyChoiceContext
  meta = emptyMetadata

-- | The `ExtraArgs` the venue supplied for one leg, or none.
extraArgsFor : TextMap ExtraArgs -> Text -> ExtraArgs
extraArgsFor supplied legId = case TextMap.lookup legId supplied of
  Some e -> e
  None   -> noExtraArgs


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AuctionMatch — ONE crossed pair, as the uncross produced it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The close allocates a quantity to each order (class → price → seqNo); this is the
-- pairing of those quantities into deliverable bilateral obligations. Nothing here
-- is a price or an allocation DECISION — those are made in MarketOnClose and this
-- record only carries the result.
--
-- `legIndex` is the pair's position in the cross, and it is what makes the two leg
-- identifiers unique. Leg ids matter: an `AllocationSpecification` carries the leg
-- id, so an allocation minted for pair 3 cannot be spent settling pair 7.
data AuctionMatch = AuctionMatch
  with
    buyer      : Party    -- receives `quantity` of the asset, pays `cashAmount`
    seller     : Party    -- delivers `quantity` of the asset, receives `cashAmount`
    quantity   : Decimal  -- units of the auctioned instrument
    cashAmount : Decimal  -- quantity * the uniform closing price, at ledger precision
    legIndex   : Int      -- position of this pair in the cross; names its two legs
  deriving (Eq, Show)

assetLegIdOf : AuctionMatch -> Text
assetLegIdOf m = "asset-" <> show m.legIndex

cashLegIdOf : AuctionMatch -> Text
cashLegIdOf m = "cash-" <> show m.legIndex

-- | The DELIVERY leg: the seller hands the asset to the buyer.
assetLegOf : TS.InstrumentId -> AuctionMatch -> TS.TransferLeg
assetLegOf assetInstrument m = TS.TransferLeg with
  sender = m.seller
  receiver = m.buyer
  amount = m.quantity
  instrumentId = assetInstrument
  meta = emptyMetadata

-- | The PAYMENT leg: the buyer pays the seller. Delivery versus payment is the two
-- of them executing in one transaction and nothing else.
cashLegOf : TS.InstrumentId -> AuctionMatch -> TS.TransferLeg
cashLegOf cashInstrument m = TS.TransferLeg with
  sender = m.buyer
  receiver = m.seller
  amount = m.cashAmount
  instrumentId = cashInstrument
  meta = emptyMetadata

-- | Both legs of one pair, keyed by leg id.
matchLegs : TS.InstrumentId -> TS.InstrumentId -> AuctionMatch -> [(Text, TS.TransferLeg)]
matchLegs assetInstrument cashInstrument m =
  [ (assetLegIdOf m, assetLegOf assetInstrument m)
  , (cashLegIdOf m,  cashLegOf cashInstrument m)
  ]

-- | The legs of a whole cross for which ONE party is the sender — i.e. exactly what
-- that party has to allocate, and nothing about anybody else's obligations.
senderLegs
  : TS.InstrumentId -> TS.InstrumentId -> [AuctionMatch] -> Party -> TextMap TS.TransferLeg
senderLegs assetInstrument cashInstrument matches p =
  TextMap.fromList
    [ (legId, leg)
    | m <- matches
    , (legId, leg) <- matchLegs assetInstrument cashInstrument m
    , leg.sender == p
    ]

-- | Every party that owes a leg in this cross.
crossSenders : [AuctionMatch] -> [Party]
crossSenders matches = dedup (concatMap (\m -> [m.buyer, m.seller]) matches)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- pairFills — turn two ALLOCATED SIDES into deliverable bilateral pairs.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The uncross tells each order how much it fills. It does NOT say who it fills
-- against, because under a uniform price nobody cares: every fill prints at the same
-- number, so which counterparty you are handed changes nothing economically. What it
-- does change is settlement, and CIP-56 settlement is bilateral — an `Allocation` is
-- assets committed to a leg between a NAMED sender and a NAMED receiver. So the
-- pairing has to be made explicit, and it has to be made by a rule rather than by
-- the venue.
--
-- THE RULE: walk both sides in the order the allocation ladder already produced them
-- (class → price → arrival sequence) and hand the head of one side to the head of the
-- other, `min` at a time. It is the same waterfall `walkQueue` uses, run against two
-- queues instead of one.
--
-- WHY THAT IS NOT A DISCRETION THE VENUE GAINS. `allocateSide` returns each side
-- SORTED by a strict total order (no two orders in a book share a `seqNo`), so both
-- input lists are already permutation-invariant with respect to the arrays the
-- operator handed `RunClose`. Pairing two canonically-ordered lists is itself
-- canonical: the same book produces byte-identical pairs no matter how the venue
-- presents it.
--
-- TOTAL, AND EXACT. Only `min` and subtraction — no division — so the pairs sum to
-- the crossed volume on both sides by construction. Termination: every step retires
-- at least one list head outright.
--
-- A SELF-MATCH IS POSSIBLE AND IS HARMLESS. A trader resting both sides of the same
-- book can be paired with itself; the resulting legs have `sender == receiver`, the
-- registry locks and returns the same holding, and no value moves. The auction has
-- never had a self-match rule and this function does not invent one.
pairFills : [(a, Decimal)] -> [(b, Decimal)] -> [(a, b, Decimal)]
pairFills [] _ = []
pairFills _ [] = []
pairFills ((x, qx) :: xs) ((y, qy) :: ys)
  | qx <= 0.0 = pairFills xs ((y, qy) :: ys)
  | qy <= 0.0 = pairFills ((x, qx) :: xs) ys
  | otherwise =
      let q   = min qx qy
          xs' = if qx > q then (x, qx - q) :: xs else xs
          ys' = if qy > q then (y, qy - q) :: ys else ys
      in (x, y, q) :: pairFills xs' ys'


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The settlement identity — built in ONE place so it cannot drift.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `SettlementInfo` is what binds an allocation to THIS close and no other. The
-- request a sender is shown, the allocation the sender creates, and the
-- specification the venue checks at settlement time are all derived from this one
-- function, so a mismatch between them is not expressible.
--
-- ONE `settlementRef` FOR THE WHOLE CROSS, and the leg id does the separating. All
-- pairs of one close share the settlement reference; `transferLegId` is unique per
-- leg per pair (`asset-3`, `cash-3`, …), and `AllocationSpecification` carries BOTH.
-- So an allocation minted for one pair cannot be redirected into another, and a
-- sender with legs in several pairs still sees a single coherent settlement.
auctionSettlementInfo : Party -> Text -> Time -> Time -> Time -> TS.SettlementInfo
auctionSettlementInfo executor settlementId requestedAt allocateBefore settleBefore =
  TS.SettlementInfo with
    executor
    settlementRef = TS.Reference with
      id = settlementId
      cid = None
    requestedAt
    allocateBefore
    settleBefore
    meta = emptyMetadata


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AuctionAllocationRequest — a CIP-56 `AllocationRequest`, ONE PER SENDER.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What a filled trader's wallet actually reads. It implements the standard
-- interface, so a CIP-56 wallet renders "you owe 4.0 cETH on leg asset-3 of
-- settlement MOC-2026-08-05, allocate by T" with no knowledge of this venue.
--
-- PRIVACY, AND THE INTERFACE EXPLICITLY BLESSES IT. `AllocationRequestView` says of
-- `transferLegs`: "This may or may not be a complete list of transfer legs that are
-- part of the settlement, depending on the confidentiality requirements of the app."
-- A dark pool's confidentiality requirement is the whole product, so each request
-- carries ONLY the legs its own trader must fund and is observed ONLY by that
-- trader. Nobody sees the cross; everybody sees their own obligations.
--
-- The venue signs alone. It has to: this contract is written inside the close, where
-- only the operator's authority is in scope. That is harmless — a request is an
-- ASKING, not an authorisation. Nothing moves because a request exists, and a
-- request the trader does not recognise is one the trader simply does not allocate
-- against.
template AuctionAllocationRequest
  with
    operator       : Party
    trader         : Party
    settlementId   : Text
    legs           : TextMap TS.TransferLeg   -- ONLY this trader's sender legs
    createdAt      : Time
    allocateBefore : Time
    settleBefore   : Time
  where
    signatory operator
    observer trader

    ensure allocateBefore <= settleBefore

    interface instance TS.AllocationRequest for AuctionAllocationRequest where
      view = TS.AllocationRequestView with
        settlement =
          auctionSettlementInfo operator settlementId createdAt allocateBefore settleBefore
        transferLegs = legs
        meta = emptyMetadata

      -- A sender signalling up front that it will not deliver. This is the ONLY
      -- artefact the pre-funded design could never produce: a signed, on-ledger,
      -- attributable record that a named party walked away from a print it bid
      -- into. The venue's remedy is off-ledger (membership, margin); the EVIDENCE
      -- is not.
      allocationRequest_RejectImpl _self arg = do
        assertMsg "only a sender of one of these legs may reject the request"
          (F.any (\l -> arg.actor == l.sender) legs)
        pure ChoiceExecutionMetadata with meta = emptyMetadata

      -- The executor stands the request down (the close was aborted). Archiving the
      -- contract is the whole effect: the ask is gone, and nothing was ever locked
      -- by it.
      allocationRequest_WithdrawImpl _self _arg =
        pure ChoiceExecutionMetadata with meta = emptyMetadata


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MatchSettlement — ONE crossed pair, ready to settle DvP.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE CONTRACT THAT CARRIES THE AUTHORITY. `Allocation_ExecuteTransfer` is
-- controlled by executor + sender + receiver, so settling a leg requires those three
-- authorities in one scope. Here they are: the operator and the two traders on this
-- pair all sign, and `MatchSettlement_Settle` is `controller operator` — which is
-- the standard's own words for the arrangement, "typically this authorization is
-- granted by sender and receiver to the executor as part of the contract
-- coordinating the settlement".
--
-- HOW THE TWO TRADERS COME TO SIGN IT, given that the close runs in a choice on the
-- auction where only the operator's authority exists. `MarketOnClose.RunClose-
-- TokenStandard` creates this with `signers = []` and then walks it through
-- `SealedOrder.CoSignSettlement` once per side. A `SealedOrder` is signed by
-- operator AND trader, so exercising a choice on it puts that trader's authority in
-- scope; declaring the already-accumulated signers as co-controllers carries the
-- earlier signature forward. Two hops, and the pair is fully signed inside the same
-- transaction that printed the cross.
--
-- WHAT THAT DOES **NOT** GIVE THE VENUE, because the shape invites the question. The
-- co-signature is scoped to a contract whose ONLY power is to execute allocations
-- that match its own two legs exactly. A venue that fabricates a `MatchSettlement`
-- and harvests signatures for it gains nothing: no allocation exists that matches a
-- specification no sender was ever shown, and `Allocation_ExecuteTransfer` is
-- unreachable without one. Compare what it replaces — a choice that moved a trader's
-- entire reserved holding on the operator's word alone.
template MatchSettlement
  with
    operator        : Party
    auditor         : Party
    -- The traders that have co-signed so far. Empty at creation; both parties to the
    -- pair by the end of the close. `MatchSettlement_Settle` refuses to run until it
    -- is complete — which is belt and braces, since without both signatures the
    -- authority to execute the allocations would not be present either.
    signers         : [Party]
    settlementId    : Text
    assetInstrument : TS.InstrumentId
    cashInstrument  : TS.InstrumentId
    closingPrice    : Decimal
    match           : AuctionMatch
    createdAt       : Time
    allocateBefore  : Time
    settleBefore    : Time
  where
    signatory operator :: signers
    -- Both traders see their own pair from the moment it is created — before either
    -- has signed — because they have to read it to allocate against it. Nobody else
    -- on the venue sees it at all, INCLUDING the auditor: the auditor's window on the
    -- close is `AuctionCross` and the `SettlementBatch`, which is where the executed
    -- book is meant to be read.
    observer match.buyer, match.seller

    ensure closingPrice > 0.0
        && match.quantity > 0.0
        && match.cashAmount > 0.0
        && allocateBefore <= settleBefore
        && length (dedup signers) == length signers
        && all (\p -> p == match.buyer || p == match.seller) signers

    -- SETTLE THIS PAIR. Both legs, one transaction, through the standard.
    -- Exercised from `AuctionCross_Settle`, which runs every pair of the close in a
    -- single transaction — so "this pair settles" and "the whole close settles" are
    -- the same event.
    choice MatchSettlement_Settle : FillRecord
      with
        assetAllocation : ContractId TS.Allocation
          -- ^ the SELLER's commitment of the asset leg
        cashAllocation  : ContractId TS.Allocation
          -- ^ the BUYER's commitment of the cash leg
        assetArgs       : ExtraArgs
          -- ^ registry choice-context for the asset leg (empty for registries that
          -- need none; NOT hardcoded, because a real one may)
        cashArgs        : ExtraArgs
      controller operator
      do
        -- Belt and braces: without both signatures the authority to exercise
        -- `Allocation_ExecuteTransfer` would not be present either, so this cannot be
        -- the only thing standing between a venue and someone's assets. It fails by
        -- NAME instead of failing as an opaque authorisation error three frames down.
        -- (Written as "every party to the pair is a signer" rather than "there are
        -- two", so a trader that crossed with itself — legal, and economically a
        -- no-op — is not locked out by an arithmetic accident.)
        assertMsg "both parties to this pair must have co-signed before it can settle"
          (match.buyer `elem` signers && match.seller `elem` signers)
        now <- getTime
        assertMsg "the settlement deadline for this cross has passed" (now < settleBefore)

        -- THE ONLY THING THE VENUE IS ALLOWED TO DO: execute the exact transfers the
        -- two senders already committed to. The whole `AllocationSpecification` is
        -- compared — settlement reference, leg id, sender, receiver, amount,
        -- instrument, deadlines — so the venue cannot substitute a different amount,
        -- a different counterparty, a different instrument, or an allocation
        -- earmarked for another pair or another close.
        assetView <- view <$> fetch @TS.Allocation assetAllocation
        assertMsg "the asset allocation does not match the leg this cross requested"
          (assetView.allocation == matchAssetSpec this)
        cashView <- view <$> fetch @TS.Allocation cashAllocation
        assertMsg "the cash allocation does not match the leg this cross requested"
          (cashView.allocation == matchCashSpec this)

        -- DELIVERY VERSUS PAYMENT. Two exercises, one transaction: there is no ledger
        -- state in which the asset moved and the cash did not.
        _ <- exercise assetAllocation TS.Allocation_ExecuteTransfer with
               extraArgs = assetArgs
        _ <- exercise cashAllocation TS.Allocation_ExecuteTransfer with
               extraArgs = cashArgs

        -- THE CONFIRM. Note what is different from the legacy close: `seller` and
        -- `buyer` are the REAL counterparties, not the venue standing in as a
        -- momentary CCP on both sides. A receipt now states who you actually traded
        -- with, because under the standard you actually traded with them.
        receipt <- create SettlementReceipt with
          seller = match.seller
          buyer = match.buyer
          venue = Some operator
          auditor
          assetInstrument = assetInstrument.id
          assetAmount = match.quantity
          cashInstrument = cashInstrument.id
          cashAmount = match.cashAmount
          unitPrice = closingPrice
          settledAt = now
        pure FillRecord with
          seller = match.seller
          buyer = match.buyer
          quantity = match.quantity
          price = closingPrice
          receipt

    -- STAND THE PAIR DOWN and hand every allocation back to the party that made it.
    -- The sender could also take its own back unilaterally (`Allocation_Withdraw` is
    -- the sender's alone) — this is the venue's tidy-up, for the case where the close
    -- as a whole cannot complete.
    choice MatchSettlement_Cancel : ()
      with
        allocations : [ContractId TS.Allocation]
        extraArgs   : ExtraArgs
      controller operator
      do
        F.forA_ allocations $ \allocCid ->
          exercise allocCid TS.Allocation_Cancel with extraArgs

-- | The exact allocation the SELLER of a pair must produce. Derived from the same
-- `auctionSettlementInfo` and the same `assetLegOf` the sender's request was built
-- from, so "what was asked for" and "what is checked at settlement" are one
-- expression evaluated twice.
matchAssetSpec : MatchSettlement -> TS.AllocationSpecification
matchAssetSpec s = TS.AllocationSpecification with
  settlement = auctionSettlementInfo
    s.operator s.settlementId s.createdAt s.allocateBefore s.settleBefore
  transferLegId = assetLegIdOf s.match
  transferLeg = assetLegOf s.assetInstrument s.match

-- | The exact allocation the BUYER of a pair must produce.
matchCashSpec : MatchSettlement -> TS.AllocationSpecification
matchCashSpec s = TS.AllocationSpecification with
  settlement = auctionSettlementInfo
    s.operator s.settlementId s.createdAt s.allocateBefore s.settleBefore
  transferLegId = cashLegIdOf s.match
  transferLeg = cashLegOf s.cashInstrument s.match


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AuctionCross — the venue's record of ONE print, and the batch executor.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Signed by the operator, observed by the auditor, and carrying no trader identities
-- at all — the pairs live on the `MatchSettlement`s, where only their own two
-- parties can read them. What this contract knows is the shape of the close: which
-- instruments, what price, how many pairs.
--
-- `matchCount` is the load-bearing field. `AuctionCross_Settle` refuses to run
-- unless it is handed EXACTLY that many distinct pairs, which is the same
-- completeness commitment `RunClose` makes about the book, made again about the
-- settlement: the venue cannot quietly settle a subset of the cross it printed.
template AuctionCross
  with
    operator        : Party
    auditor         : Party
    settlementId    : Text
    assetInstrument : TS.InstrumentId
    cashInstrument  : TS.InstrumentId
    closingPrice    : Decimal
    matchCount      : Int
    crossedQty      : Decimal   -- total units this print crossed, across all pairs
    participants    : [Party]   -- the book's roster, for the batch record
    createdAt       : Time
    allocateBefore  : Time
    settleBefore    : Time
  where
    signatory operator
    observer auditor

    ensure closingPrice > 0.0
        && matchCount > 0
        && crossedQty > 0.0
        && allocateBefore <= settleBefore

    -- SETTLE THE WHOLE CROSS. Every pair, every leg, one Daml transaction.
    --
    -- Each `MatchSettlement_Settle` below runs with ITS OWN pair's authority (the
    -- operator plus that pair's two traders), which is how a venue that is a
    -- signatory of nothing but its own records can execute allocations controlled by
    -- parties it cannot otherwise act for. If any leg fails a check — a missing
    -- allocation, a substituted amount, an expired deadline — the whole close rolls
    -- back and NOTHING settles.
    choice AuctionCross_Settle : ContractId SettlementBatch
      with
        legs : [MatchSettleInstruction]
      controller operator
      do
        now <- getTime
        assertMsg "the settlement deadline for this cross has passed" (now < settleBefore)
        assertMsg "every matched pair of this cross must settle in the same transaction"
          (length legs == matchCount)
        assertMsg "the same pair was supplied more than once"
          (length (dedup (map (\l -> l.matchCid) legs)) == length legs)

        fills <- forA legs $ \l ->
          exercise l.matchCid MatchSettlement_Settle with
            assetAllocation = l.assetAllocation
            cashAllocation = l.cashAllocation
            assetArgs = extraArgsFor l.legArgs "asset"
            cashArgs = extraArgsFor l.legArgs "cash"

        assertMsg "the settled volume does not equal the printed cross"
          (sum (map (\f -> f.quantity) fills) == crossedQty)

        create SettlementBatch with
          operator; auditor
          instrumentId = assetInstrument.id
          closingPrice
          fills
          traders = participants
          settledAt = now

    -- ABORT. The close printed but cannot settle — a sender rejected, a deadline is
    -- about to pass, a registry is unreachable. Every allocation returns to the party
    -- that made it, and nobody is left with assets locked behind a settlement that is
    -- never going to happen. Nothing was ever taken, so there is nothing to give
    -- back beyond the locks.
    choice AuctionCross_Abort : ()
      with
        pairs : [MatchAbortInstruction]
      controller operator
      do
        F.forA_ pairs $ \p ->
          exercise p.matchCid MatchSettlement_Cancel with
            allocations = p.allocations
            extraArgs = p.extraArgs

-- | One pair's settlement instruction: the pair, its two allocations, and any
-- registry-supplied choice context. `legArgs` is keyed by `"asset"` / `"cash"`;
-- an absent key means "this registry needs no context for that leg", which is the
-- ordinary case and the reason it is a map rather than two required fields.
data MatchSettleInstruction = MatchSettleInstruction
  with
    matchCid        : ContractId MatchSettlement
    assetAllocation : ContractId TS.Allocation
    cashAllocation  : ContractId TS.Allocation
    legArgs         : TextMap ExtraArgs
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | One pair's abort instruction: whichever allocations exist for it (possibly
-- none, if that pair's senders never allocated).
data MatchAbortInstruction = MatchAbortInstruction
  with
    matchCid    : ContractId MatchSettlement
    allocations : [ContractId TS.Allocation]
    extraArgs   : ExtraArgs
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | What `MarketOnClose.RunCloseTokenStandard` hands back: the venue's record of the
-- print, the per-pair settlements the two sides must allocate against, and the
-- per-sender allocation requests their wallets will read.
data AuctionCloseResult = AuctionCloseResult
  with
    cross    : ContractId AuctionCross
    matches  : [ContractId MatchSettlement]
    requests : [ContractId AuctionAllocationRequest]
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Look one leg's specification up by id out of a whole cross. Used by tests and
-- by any wallet-side helper that has the request in hand.
legSpecOf
  : Party -> Text -> Time -> Time -> Time -> TextMap TS.TransferLeg -> Text
  -> TS.AllocationSpecification
legSpecOf executor settlementId createdAt allocateBefore settleBefore legs legId =
  TS.AllocationSpecification with
    settlement = auctionSettlementInfo executor settlementId createdAt allocateBefore settleBefore
    transferLegId = legId
    transferLeg = fromSomeNote
      ("no such leg in this allocation request: " <> legId)
      (TextMap.lookup legId legs)
